          "value": "3-Tier",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Base Level",
          "value": "Gram Sabha",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Apex Level",
          "value": "Zila Parishad",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "owqvizq"
    },
    {
      "id": "3pr1yap",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Background of Panchayati Raj"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Constitutional provisions for the Panchayati Raj System are contained in Part IX of the Constitution.",
        "The Panchayati Raj System operates on a Three-Tier Structure across rural India.",
        "Gram Sabha serves as the fundamental unit of democratic local governance.",
        "The system progresses from Gram Panchayat at the village level to Panchayat Samiti at the block level and Zila Parishad at the district level."
      ],
      "id": "nvqdtnj"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "02f55ja",
      "content": "• Village self-government existed in ancient India."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2far7rq",
      "content": "• The Balwant Rai Mehta Committee (1957) recommended democratic decentralization."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kvm0ct2",
      "content": "• The committee suggested a three-tier Panchayati Raj system."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "26na9o7",
      "content": "• The 73rd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992 gave constitutional status to Panchayats."
    },
    {
